e essentially been  applied  uh with different compounds and  each one of these cell types essentially  um you can see kind of breaks off into  its separate  uh cluster meaning that that  transcriptome profile  is is somewhat similar within a  particular cell line  but if you then focus your analysis  within each cell line you can actually  see that different compounds  at different concentrations will  actually give you a differential  expression or a different transcriptome  profile and this is what basically  creates the signature essentially that  somebody can then  use to evaluate a new test compound  compared to  standards or known compounds that have  previously been run and if you look at  the top  left panel here you can see very clearly  if you focus on this dmso control it's  actually the blue that's over here in  the left  cluster along with two other compounds  and so  the dmso is the vehicle control to get  the compounds essentially into the cells  so in two of the compounds here we're  not getting much of a transcriptome  response here but you can see these  other three compounds are actually  giving us a very unique transcriptome  profile  characteristic of of those compounds  this is quite a powerful way  to essentially look at this data now  again  the beautiful thing about go screen is  that we're looking at 20  000 gene measurements and previous  technologies  if you're thinking about using things  like biospider  i think they use about 3500 different  gene expression profiles  or if you look at the l1000 technology  the  luminex bead technology that's only  looking at a thousand genes  here we're able to actually focus a  transcriptome profile on 20  000 genes and this is really the power  of of this particular  platform one thing i also want to  introduce you to  is something called dimensional  reduction algorithms  uh here's an example of uh some data  using something we call ega or  exploratory grouping analysis and this  comes in the tax software  what's nice about these dimensional  reduction algorithms  is that it goes and it looks for  clusters  of transcriptome profiles essentially so  it helps you build  essentially this structure of your data  and allows you to then find similarities  based on other things you know about the  compounds or other  responses that you've collected over the  course of your  your history doing any any type of  screening or clinical trials  uh this particular uh tool we're using  is called t stay  tuesday is quite popular these days it's  used for  all kinds of things for different for  looking at patterns in traffic  as well as internet usage patterns  and things like that so these are quite  powerful algorithms and when you can  take  20 000 pieces of information for every  compound and throw at these particular  algorithms it can be quite powerful  in finding associations um  just to be clear when we use these  different algorithms or incorporate them  in our software we always have an  advanced parameter  mode so if you're a bioinformatics  person out there  these tools although they're quite easy  for a normal person to use  you can also get quite sophisticated in  your analysis by using the advanced  parameters  that we've also made available  so originally a lot of this work was  done  using something called the connectivity  map or c  map and it's a it's a really brilliant  resource that was originally constructed  by the broad  which uses transcriptional expression  data to basically look at  all of these relationships between  diseases cell physiology and different  therapeutics and and these signatures  essentially are sitting there and and  can be used today  uh to essentially go and profile uh  compounds um  there's over 100 different cell types in  the database and there's over 27 000  different compound concentration  combinations that are in there  now this database was only generated  really across a thousand genes and these  were landmark genes  that 