nd screening with them  this this is the array that you would  want to use  now the go in in clarion go screen  is actually referring to the gene  ontology resource and this is a very  important database  for many of our pharmaceutical partners  they essentially are able to break out  the gene content of our transcriptomes  and use this resource  to get information in terms of the  cellular components the biological  processes  and the molecular functions of the genes  that we've represented  on on these particular microarrays  now the application that  is most used today for these ghost  screen products is  pharma secondary compound library  screening which is essentially hit  profiling  so what happens is these pharmaceutical  companies are taking these vast  databases of compounds and they're  profiling them  on various cellular assays and they get  in the order of tens of thousands of  hits  basically from these various compounds  it's then important to get more  information  on these hips and they use  systems like ghost green to essentially  understand  uh the the profile of the gene  expression that is actually coming  from that particular screen now other  customers have been interested in using  these arrays for  other applications such as studying  immunology  and inflammatory disease and we have  customers who are actually taking  patient derived cells  uh they're sorting the cells uh and then  they're profiling these cells in  cellular assays  uh again using different compounds but a  much smaller number of compounds  and these uh these types of studies can  basically help identify  uh responders and non non-responders as  well as patients who are undergoing some  type of adverse effect  and allows you to kind of go in and  study uh more importantly what's  happening  in these particular immunology  inflammation cell types  if you think of 12 different individuals  who might be  studied here you might sort those 12  patients out into  12 different sorted cells and you might  then take  each different sorted cell and profile  it with different compounds and so these  experiments can explode up into large  numbers  very very quickly and that's why this  high throughput system works very well  um the rad product that we have out is  actually being looked at  in terms of using it for toxicology  specifically mechanistic toxicology  and you can imagine that looking at  these gene expression profiles can  actually help you quickly identify  the toxicity mechanisms  that are happening in certain types of  uh uh events  uh within these animals that are being  tested um we've had people also contact  us about egg bio screening  uh and these model screening  applications as well  again the idea here is we're working  with tens of thousands of samples  and can provide up to 20 000 profiles at  a very low cost  now using gene expression profiles to  classify and stratify compounds  is actually something uh that has uh  been validated and understood for quite  some time  uh here's a paper that was actually uh  developed by a group  uh from bear scientific david work as  one of the authors on here  but they were one of the first people to  actually show that you can actually  train uh a a system  like this to develop a signature or  profile for these various compounds  and that allows you then to essentially  predict the response you might get or  the profile  you might get from new or novel  compounds  and this is quite valuable to be able to  actually  do this in some type of screening as  opposed to trying to develop this  and test in animals or or humans  obviously  so one of the main things we've had to  do to develop this system is to fit  essentially 20 000 genes  on a very tiny peg that we can put into  our high throughput system  the way we went about this was we  started with our our older systems  and looked at our clarium s and clarium  d platforms  uh here typically we're looking at  around 10 probes per gene and what you  see here on the right  is essentially a gene model there's many  different transcript isoforms that can  be spliced  in different ways and you can create  essentially a gene model looking at all  these different exons of this particular  gene  what we typically do is we would put  down the 10  best probes in various exons across this  gene model  to get essentially a summary of the gene  expression event that was happening here  for this gene  now to get a smaller uh chip  basically to reduce the cost what we've  done here is we focus the content on the  best  three probes and we've done this by  doing uh both  probe based selection uh using in silico  ranking  and and empirical probe selection using  real data  that we've accumulated over the years on  these various micro arrays  notice that we're also trying to target  probes in the most constitutive regions  here of the gene  so basically we're trying to get a gene  level estimate as opposed to trying to  understand the splicing  and the differences of the exons here  when we go and we look at the  performance in a classical latin square  study using spikes  we can compare this performance  essentially to our other products what  you see here is our clarium  s gene level product compared to the  human go screen products at both using  all 10 probes  and using the three probes that we've  selected and essentially  we get the same performance in terms of  sensitivity and specificity  now these pots that you're looking at  here these are called receiver operator  characteristic curves and again what  this is telling us is that we can  actually look  at a very low level event here this is  actually looking at one and a half  copies per cell versus three copies per  cell  on average in an rna sample okay and so  this is looking at a very low  differential expression change and what  you see here on the right panel are  those spikes  and you see a blue distribution of the  of the spikes this is the t  statistic or t test saying yes i see it  differentially expressed  versus everything else on the microarray  which is the null distribution it should  not be changing  that's all of the other genes that  aren't spikes this is a very nice way  for us to clearly look at  and and gauge essentially the  performance of these microarrays so  we've picked essentially the best three  probes and we achieve a performance  level  uh that we're satisfied with for these  types of applications  now if you ask how does this compare to  other technologies  uh like rna sequencing well we can  actually take  um the same rna samples that were used  during the microarray quality control  consortium  and were essentially applied to both  tacman and  rna sequencing and we can show that the  full change correlations are essentially  equivalent to what we get with these  other technologies  using purified rna so again we expect  these arrays to work  much like these other technologies and  being able to find  differentially expressed genes  now the clarium doe screen arrays also  contain  a lot of controls we have the ability to  look at this the general signal metrics  to make sure we're getting out  a good signal from the the genes we  expect to see expressed  we also have controls for the  hybridization  this is both the array and the scanning  controls and then we have target prep  controls which actually  make sure that the rna was in fact  purified  and or amplified correctly in addition  to that we have these very nice positive  negative controls these are actually  exon intron controls  and we expect to get good signal from  the exons obviously  and no signal from the introns if we  start to see signal from the introns  that means we're amplifying dna and that  rna  and that would be problematic so this is  a nice set of controls that we also have  on the array  um we have what we call anti-genomic  controls as well these are  probes that are used to estimate signal  background and can be  removed uh you help to remove the  background from  uh uh your probes during the pipeline  processing that happens  as i mentioned earlier we also have the  viral content on here for the human  genes  and we've included the ercc controls  that were made uh popular  during the microarray quality control  consortium  the good news is you don't really have  to worry about these controls because  your service provider  will look at these controls and use them  to make sure that the assay is working  correctly  and more importantly you can actually  analyze this data  in our software which is transcriptome  analysis console or we call this tac  we've designed this for investigators  although it's very sophisticated and a  lot of bioinformatics people  do use this um if you're not familiar  with this it's one of the biggest  advancements we've had  in our array portfolio it's free  it works with our new and older arrays  and basically it allows you to retain  that ownership of your data so that you  can actually analyze it  and get your results and move on uh much  quicker with your research  there's already over 600 literature  citations where they've published papers  essentially using the software and using  the figures that come out of the  software  and down below here you can see some  very nice figures  we have the principal component analysis  the differential expression analysis and  the ability to do things like  hierarchical clustering  uh if you're not familiar with this with  some of our other products you can also  do  micro rna association analysis as well  and pathway analysis  now when we're working specifically with  ghost green  what we're really trying to do is we're  trying to understand  what the transcriptome profile is for a  particular compound in a particular cell  type  and what i'm showing you here is  actually some real data  and then in the center uh the large pca  plot that you see here  there's actually uh six different  cell types that have essentially been  applied  uh with different compounds and  each one of these cell types essentially  um you can see kind of breaks off into  its separate  uh cluster meaning that that  transcriptome profile  is is somewhat similar within a  particular cell line  but if you then focus your analysis  within each cell line you can actually  see that different compounds  at different concentrations will  actually give you a differential  expression or a different transcriptome  profile and this is what basically  creates the signature essentially that  somebody can then  use to evaluate a new test compound  compared to  standards or known compounds that have  previously been run and if you look at  the top  left panel here you can see very clearly  if you focus on this dmso control it's  actually the blue that's over here in  the left  cluster along with two other compounds  and so  the dmso is the vehicle control to get  the compounds essentially into the cells  so in two of the compounds here we're  not getting much of a transcriptome  response here but you can see these  other three compounds are actually  giving us a very unique transcriptome  profile  characteristic of of those compounds  this is quite a powerful way  to essentially look at this data now  again  the beautiful thing about go screen is  that we're looking at 20  000 gene measurements and previous  technologies  if you're thinking about using things  like biospider  i think they use about 3500 different  gene expression profiles  or if you look at the l1000 technology  the  luminex bead technology that's only  looking at a thousand genes  here we're able to actually focus a  transcriptome profile on 20  000 genes and this is really the power  of of this particular  platform one thing i also want to  introduce you to  is something called dimensional  reduction algorithms  uh here's an example of uh some data  using something we call ega or  exploratory grouping analysis and this  comes in the tax software  what's nice about these dimensional  reduction algorithms  is that it goes and it looks for  clusters  of transcriptome profiles essentially so  it helps you build  essentially this structure of your data  and allows you to then find similarities  based on other things you know about the  compounds or other  responses that you've collected over the  course of your  your history doing any any type of  screening or clinical trials  uh this particular uh tool we're using  is called t stay  tuesday is quite popular these days it's  used for  all kinds of things for different for  looking at patterns in traffic  as well as internet usage patterns  and things like that so these are quite  powerful algorithms and when you can  take  20 000 pieces of information for every  compound and throw at these particular  algorithms it can be quite powerful  in finding associations um  just to be clear when we use these  different algorithms or incorporate them  in our software we always have an  advanced parameter  mode so if you're a bioinformatics  person out there  these tools although they're quite easy  for a normal person to use  you can also get quite sophisticated in  your analysis by using the advanced  parameters  that we've also made available  so originally a lot of this work was  done  using something called the connectivity  map or c  map and it's a it's a really brilliant  resource that was originally constructed  by the broad  which uses transcriptional expression  data to basically look at  all of these relationships between  diseases cell physiology and different  therapeutics and and these signatures  essentially are sitting there and and  can be used today  uh to essentially go and profile uh  compounds um  there's over 100 different cell types in  the database and there's over 27 000  different compound concentration  combinations that are in there  now this database was only generated  really across a thousand genes and these  were landmark genes  that were originally actually found on  microarrays  and thought to be a the best  representation  of of genes so basically to get the cost  down  they used this luminex bead technology  to profile a thousand genes here  and what's been found uh is that this  thousand genes  is was probably selected uh tailored  more for cancer  than for these other types of screens  and so the thought is is  what would happen if we actually go and  use 20 000 genes as opposed to just  a thousand genes however  this data resource still exists out  there  and people still want to leverage this  vast resource and so  if we want to we can actually go and try  to compare now  go screen data to cmap and the profiles  that are in cmap  now to be able to do that you want to  use what's called the level 5 c  map data and what i'm showing you here  is that you can actually take the  differences between the compounds  and the vehicle control in these screens  and these gene expression profile  differences  are actually very similar between the  two platforms and so  the triangles here actually represent uh  ghost green  um and this particular compound that's  highlighted here it's kind of the salmon  uh pink color here this is compound  ap7519  you can see that these profiles are all  clustered together here  and within this profile you're going to  there's different cell types which is  why you see the separation that you see  here  now there is some technical variance you  can see in the different platforms  however what's good here is that you can  still leverage and get some  estimation of what your compound looks  like relative to  the data that exists in cmap in fact  you can actually build a prediction  based on an enrichment scoring  technique and when we did this  with these six compounds across these  five different  cell types that you see at the top here  it turns out we could actually correctly  predict in most cases  the top prediction was both the correct  cell type and drug  in 16 out of the 34 cases using  this enrichment score  algorithm essentially now in some cases  um we didn't that wasn't  our our top hit was not the actual  compound  uh however it was um often  uh within the first two or three  compounds  um that we would uh uh uh  assay essentially um in terms of the  hits  um and then you have these other cases  where in fact you see the same mechanism  for a different compound that is  essentially known to have a  the same effect essentially by the same  mechanism  which essentially translates down to the  same pathway and that's what we see here  uh in this case with a persimmon tab  here  now if we um really want to talk about  screening it's really important to  understand  that we've enabled this particular  technology to work with direct lysates  and this is new for microarrays  typically we only work with purified rna  we've designed this platform to work  with something called  cells to signal which is a  commercially available direct lysis  solution basically it's already been  tested and works with a number of  different cell lines  most of our testing on this particular  platform is actually done  with gila and hep g2 lysates  and to understand how this works in your  lab  typically when you're running these  screens you're working with 96 well  plates  or or even smaller plates or in some  cases larger plates but the idea here is  that you want to  directly lyse these cells and to get a  good lysate that works with our assay  you want to make sure that you do an ice  cold pbs wash  on those cells and then what you want to  do is you want to basically target a  concentration of 100 cells per  microliter  using this lysis buffer the different  screens will have different  cells and the cells themselves would be  of different sizes  and so what we will typically do is  we'll work with the customer to  make sure ensure that this license  process is happening efficiently  with that customer but again the two  main important things  here are you want to make sure you do  this very cold pbs wash with the samples  to remove all the media  and then you want to target your lysis  solution on the cells  at about 100 cells per microliter  when you go and you actually uh send  your samples to the service provider  you're going to be sending them  uh probably in a 96 well plate if you're  already working in 384 well plates you  can do that as well  but we do request that you leave a  couple of walls blank so that we can add  some hela and liver rna controls and  these are batch processing controls  that allow us to see essentially what's  happening at the service provider  and when we talk about actually working  with a partner such as yourself  typically we go through these different  phases there's a pilot phase  here's again where we like to determine  that the cell line is actually working  with the lysis  under the right conditions in some cases  we might actually  do a second dilution in the lysis buffer  um and we might also adjust the ampli  amplification cycles that we're actually  using  should we be working with some very  different  type of cell this allows us to ensure  basically that everything is working  well up front then typically there'll be  a small  pop study where the customer will will  run  known compounds with known effects so  that we can uh basically demonstrate  uh the effectiveness of these  transcriptome profiles and we do all of  this again  ahead of the full screen and again these  screens can be quite large we're talking  uh in order of 10 uh to 40 000  samples in a screen um and that allows  us essentially to get down to  these very low cost price points  now we've partnered essentially this  service offering with ibx  here in the united states this is  formerly known as redcore  or or this was uh the rutgers  service uh provider they've recently  changed their name uh  to infinity biologics ibx uh however and  they are  in uh in new jersey uh so in the united  states this is your  primary service provider uh and if  you're  listening to this recording from europe  uh we've partnered  with eurofins uh formerly known as  aerosupplied biotechnology out of  denmark now both of these groups  uh we've worked with quite extensively  um and we really value their  participation in this platform because  they've been  uh really great partners and generate  really high quality data  what uh typically a customer will do is  they'll actually order this  lysis buffer and then they will  work with us and send essentially their  samples  to these service providers uh who will  then buy the arrays and the chips  and and and run the actual service  so with that i will thank you for your  attention and take any questions  thank you so much anthony for that  outstanding presentation we're going to  go ahead and move into the live q a  but we are running on short on time so  we'll probably only have time for that  one question  so the first one i have here is when  compared to the alternative screening  platforms like  rna-seq why is go screen the best choice  for research  maggie thinks that's a that's a fairly  common question we get and  the answer is is twofold one is cost  and quite simply even though the cost of  bulk rna sequencing has come down  quite a bit the cost of doing library  construction  especially thinking about doing library  construction across tens of thousands of  samples  is quite prohibitive the other thing is  that we're still working with rna which  means you need  dynamic range you need to make sure that  these signals or these profiles that you  get  if you're measuring uh gene x in in one  transcriptome profile it might be  represented 10 000 times  where gene y might be there five times  and then in another sample  gene x and gene y might be there five  times uh  for for both genes and so the the number  of reads you need to see those genes can  change quite a bit and so  you need a minimum depth essentially to  see  those gene expression profiles  and when you put all of that together  across 20 000 genes  and try to do it with sequencing that  depth becomes quite prohibitive  now if you try to target it with panels  first of all it's quite difficult to put  together panels of that size of 20 000  but more importantly across these large  screens  if you're building databases across time  it becomes prohibitive because these  these panels that you build have  variation to them  uh in their manufacturing and so you'll  have dropouts and things like that that  happen  what's nice about microarrays is that  semiconductor technology  creates essentially the same microarray  every time so it's really great for  these studies  uh that that go on uh for long periods  of time that that basically  are building uh databases so it allows  you to directly measure  uh all of these 20 000 events on every  single chip  you don't have to worry about drop out  thanks so much anthony and thank you to  everyone in the audience for the great  questions today  as we have run out of time just a  reminder any questions you did submit  that were not answered will be addressed  via email  and don't miss out on all of our other  valuable presentations as part of this  virtual event you can visit the agenda  tab in the auditorium for  full listing to receive more information  about the new clarium ghost green essay  please visit our microarray analysis  booth in the exhibit hall  the poster hall to see ghost screen  posters or click on the red request more  information button throughout the  virtual event  lastly this presentation will be  available for on-demand viewing through  september 30th 2021.  please share it with a colleague who  might be interested thank you all again  for your participation  until next time goodbye
 

